                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a cloven fibration.
          %h3#other_definitions Other definitions
          %p
            Most other characterizations of Grothendieck fibrations can also be given in a way which does not violate the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            to characterize Street fibrations.
          %h2#comparison_to_grothendieck_fibrations Comparison to Grothendieck fibrations
          %p
            Every Grothendieck fibration is a Street fibration, as is every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            (the latter are not in general Grothendieck fibrations). Since Street fibrations are closed under composition, any functor equivalent to a Street fibration is also a Street fibration (this is the “covariance” property). As remarked above, however, almost all functors in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            which one wants to treat as “fibrations” are actually Grothendieck fibrations. Moreover, in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            every Street fibration is the composite of an equivalence with a Grothendieck fibration: the latter can be taken to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement"} fibrant replacement
            of it in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            on Cat. (In fact, a Street fibration is a Grothendieck fibration precisely when it is also a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"} isofibration
          %p
            For these reasons, Street fibrations in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            are little-studied. The fact that they exist, and that any Street fibration is equivalent to a Grothendieck one, can be useful in assuaging philosophical worries about violation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            in the notion of Grothendieck fibration, but in practice in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            one usually wants to use Grothendieck fibrations.
          %p
            However, when internalizing in other 2-categories, covariance becomes more important. This is most obvious when the 2-category in which one wishes to internalize is only a weak 2-category (a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            if we define it using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctors"} anafunctors
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            = succeed ",\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biequivalence"} biequivalence
          %p
            For example, this is arguably the case in the 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} morphisms
            to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left-exact
            left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoints
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
            having a left-exact left adjoint, or adjoint pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f^*,f_*)
            in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            is left exact. The three choices give three strict 2-categories which are all biequivalent, but not strictly 2-equivalent; thus it is more sensible to consider internal Street fibrations in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Topos
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Topos
            than strict Grothendieck ones.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p Since Street fibrations are the “minimal covariant modification” of Grothendieck ones, any property enjoyed by Grothendieck fibrations which is itself covariant under equivalence will also be possessed by Street fibrations.
          %p
            One thing to note is that in general, when working with Street fibrations, one must always replace
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+fibers"} essential fibers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon J\to I
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to induce a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^*\colon E^I\to E^J
            between strict fibers, but such a functor is induced between essential fibers. In this way we can reconstruct a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"} pseudofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^{op}\to Cat
            from any Street fibration, and show that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of Street fibrations is equivalent to that of such pseudofunctors (though not, as in the case of Grothendieck fibrations,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-equivalence+of+2-categories"} strictly 2-equivalent
          %p
            Just as every Grothendieck fibration is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strictly+exponentiable+functor"} strictly exponentiable functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponentiable morphism
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            Cat.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Of course, any Grothendieck fibration is a Street fibration, but the interest is in those which are not Grothendieck. Some of the applications are internal to 2-categories other than
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The original motivation was to consider fibrations (and opfibrations, and
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-sided+fibrations"} two-sided fibrations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %msup
                        %mi Cat
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat^{op}
                as a means to construct
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
            %li
              %p
                Street fibrations in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
                are discussed in the paper of Johnstone below.
          %p
            Street fibrations in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            also arise in mathematics, though often under different names. The following examples were pointed out by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Janelidze"} George Janelidze
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"} Steve Lack
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The notion of “admissible object” in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+Galois+theory"} categorical Galois theory
                can be expressed in terms of a functor being (partially) a Street fibration, not generally Grothendieck.
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left exact
                (or, more generally, “semi-left exact”)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflection"} reflection
                is a Street fibration, not generally Grothendieck.
          %h2#references References
          %p The notion was originally defined in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fibrations in bicategories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/article/CTGDC_1980__21_2_111_0.pdf"} pdf
          %p although there it was given in a very abstract form. A more explicit form like that given above can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              Ross Street,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Conspectus of variable categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022404981900219/pdf?md5=3d791b95c31feb34887b5144975c2f02&pid=1-s2.0-0022404981900219-main.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Some alternate characterizations, and applications in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Fibrations and partial products in a 2-category
          %p The proof that a semi-left-exact reflection is a Street fibration can be found in Section 5 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matias+Menni"}> Matias Menni
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Decidable objects and molecular toposes
